  <h2>Context</h2>
  <p>
    This is early work — a student project from 2023, before any of the commercial
    SEO or automation work. I keep it public deliberately. The trajectory matters
    more than pretending it started polished.
  </p>

  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p>
    An application for logging and tracking bike-theft cases: report a theft, list
    and filter existing cases, and update their status. Different roles see
    different things, which is where the interesting part was.
  </p>

  <h2>What I learned from it</h2>
  <p>
    This was the first project where I worked against a real authenticated API
    instead of local state or a mock. Three things landed here that I have used
    ever since:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Auth is a state problem, not a screen.</strong> The login form was
      the easy half. Holding a token, refreshing it, deciding what the UI does
      while auth is unresolved, and handling the request that fails <em>because</em>
      the token expired mid-session — that is where the actual work was.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Role-based access has to exist on both sides.</strong> Hiding a
      control in the interface is presentation, not permission. Learning that the
      client-side check is a convenience and the server-side check is the actual
      boundary was worth the whole project.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Error states are most of the surface area.</strong> The happy path
      was a fraction of the code by the time it behaved reasonably.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Status</h2>
  <p>
    Archived. It is not code I would write today, which is roughly the point of
    leaving it up.
  </p>
</Work>
